"""tunegame - the deep dive on one title, on this Mac, saved for everyone.

    tunegame run    --disc-id <id> [--level 1|2] [--budget-minutes <n>]
                    [--play-seconds <n>] [--variants a,b,..] [--fresh] [--no-record]
    tunegame all    [--level 1|2] [--budget-minutes <n>] [--hours <n>]
                    [--only-untuned] [--target-speed <x>] [--agent]
    tunegame status
    tunegame report --disc-id <id>
    tunegame bench  --disc-id <id> --module <dylib> [--state <sav>] [--seconds <n>]
                    [--env K=V]...
    tunegame ab     --disc-id <id> --a <dylib> --b <dylib> [--state <sav>]
                    [--pairs <n>] [--seconds <n>] [--env K=V]...
    tunegame build  --disc-id <id> --out <dir> [--chunk <n>]
                    [--pgo none|gen|scene|<.profdata>] [--env K=V]...
    tunegame add    --iso <image>...
    tunegame clean  [--disc-id <id>]... [--keep-tuned]

`recompios send` already profiles every game for four minutes with a generic
button-masher before it is recompiled for the phone. That is the floor. This
is the ceiling: what a developer did by hand for the first title that ran
well on an iPhone, made repeatable, so the result can be written into the
repository and every later send of that title -- on any Mac -- starts from
it. Everything happens on this Mac, headless, with the same runtime the
desktop app uses standing in for the phone (no fallback JIT, limiter off).
The phone is never touched.

`run` on one title:

  level 1 (about 20 minutes a game)
    1. recompile the DOL instrumented, for this Mac
    2. play it from boot with scripted input, writing a savestate every 20 s
       and the speed every 2 s
    3. rank the states by the speed around them and bench the slowest three;
       the heaviest healthy one becomes the title's bench scene
    4. play from that scene for two more minutes, instrumented, and merge
       both runs into one profile weighted toward the scene
    5. recompile against that profile, then run the scene with the sampler
       on: where host time goes, where the module returns to the chassis,
       which guest functions dominate, what the chassis had to skip or lose
    6. write the record and a report with the leads a person or an agent
       should look at next

  level 2 (each variant is a recompile and an interleaved A/B, minutes to a
  quarter of an hour, until --budget-minutes runs out)
    generic   the profile recompios would have collected on its own
    plain     no profile at all
    chunk128, chunk32   other function sizes, each with its own profile
  A variant that beats the tuned module with arms that do not overlap is
  adopted into the record.

What goes where:

  DolBundler/tuning/<ID>/      the record, committed: tuning.conf (what to
                               build with), profile.profdata.xz, report.md
  ~/Library/Application Support/DolBundler/tuning/<ID>/
                               the working store: the bench scene savestate,
                               the tuned module, every log, results.json
  DolBundler/tuning/SUMMARY.md the library at a glance, rewritten by `all`
                               and `status`

`recompios send` reads the record: a title with one is recompiled for the
phone against the committed profile and settings instead of a fresh
four-minute collection, and says so. `all` is the overnight loop: every
library game in turn, untuned first, then stale, then slowest, until --hours
is up; with --agent, a `claude -p /tune-games <ID>` pass follows each title
the report marks as needing attention. `bench`, `ab` and `build` are the
same measurements on their own, for a person or an agent chasing a lead.

Measurement rules this script keeps so you do not have to (all learned the
hard way; see DolBundler/tuning/README.md): never bench with another
moderngekko-run alive; A/B interleaved and back to back, never against a
number from earlier; a scene that reads far faster than the rest of the run
is a guest that has crashed, not a fast scene; a savestate has to be re-benched
for faults before it is trusted; a profile with a total count of zero is worse
than none.
"""

